/* ==========================================================================
   Likely on the exam: Dr. Parsons' half (15 of 30 questions). Twenty hard
   "star" items built from the verified Week 3 (OTC insect stings and head
   lice), Week 5 (physical assessment and POCT) and Week 6 (immunizations)
   material. Sources: OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf, APhA-Insect-Excerpt.pdf,
   POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf, Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf,
   AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf (Notes, Appendix, Tables 1 and 2) and the 9/21
   transcript marked (T). Items use patient cases, two-step reasoning and
   traps (a precaution is not a contraindication; hepatitis A vaccine is not
   live; 5 days early is invalid but 4 days early is valid).
   Where the sources differ: slide 27 says hydrocortisone 1% is applied 1-4
   times a day; the APhA excerpt says 3 or 4 times daily. No item keys the
   frequency.
   ========================================================================== */
TOPICS.unshift({id:'starp', name:'★ Likely on the exam: Dr. Parsons', prof:'Dr. Parsons', open:true, cite:'OTC, POCT and immunization decks and the AAFP schedule', subs:[
  {id:'otc', name:'OTC self-care', cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 8–10, 14–34; APhA-Insect-Excerpt.pdf'},
  {id:'poct', name:'Physical assessment and POCT', cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 30–58'},
  {id:'imm', name:'Immunizations', cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf; AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf; 9/21 transcript'}]});

QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---- OTC self-care ---------------------------------------------------- */
{id:'sp01', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-sting-exclusion-case',
 tags:['star','stings','exclusions'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A 31-year-old woman was stung on the forearm by a yellow jacket 20 minutes ago. She has pain, itching and a 3 cm area of redness at the sting site, and she now feels slightly dizzy. She takes no medications and has no allergies. Her father has hay fever, and a wasp sting last summer caused only local itching. Which findings exclude her from self-care? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Pain, itching and 3 cm of redness at the site', correct:false, why:'Pain, itching and irritation at the site are the usual complaint treated in self-care (slide 20), and no item on slide 22 covers redness limited to the sting site. Choosing it mistakes a normal local reaction for an allergic one and would refer a patient for no reason.'},
  {t:'Dizziness that began after the sting', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 lists dizziness, with hives, excessive swelling, weakness, nausea, vomiting and difficulty breathing, as an exclusion, and slide 14 refers a sting with systemic symptoms. Slide 19 adds that anaphylaxis can appear as soon as 10–15 minutes and up to 6 hours after the sting, and lists light-headedness, so dizziness at 20 minutes is treated as a reaction away from the site, not as a minor symptom.'},
  {t:'A father with hay fever', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 lists a personal or family history of significant allergic reactions such as hay fever as an exclusion, so her father’s hay fever counts even though she has no allergies herself. Ignoring it reveals the confusion that only the patient’s own allergy history matters; this is the least obvious exclusion in the case.'},
  {t:'Only local itching after the sting last summer', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 excludes severe reactions to previous insect bites or stings, and itching limited to the site is a local reaction, not a severe one. Choosing it confuses any past reaction with a severe past reaction, which would exclude almost every adult who has been stung before.'},
  {t:'A sting from a yellow jacket rather than a honeybee', correct:false, why:'Slide 18 lists honeybees, wasps, hornets, yellow jackets and fire ants as venomous insects, and slides 21–22 do not make any one of them an exclusion. Choosing it confuses the type of insect with the type of reaction, which is what decides self-care versus referral.'}],
 teach:'Self-care for a sting needs only local pain, itching and irritation at the site (slide 20). Refer to urgent care or the ER for any finding on slide 22: hives, excessive swelling, dizziness, weakness, nausea, vomiting, difficulty breathing, an allergic response away from the sting, a severe reaction to a previous sting, a personal or family history of significant allergic reactions such as hay fever, or age under 2 years. Quick method: sort the history into the patient’s symptoms now, past stings and family allergy history, and check each group against slide 22. Anaphylaxis can start 10–15 minutes to 6 hours after a sting, so counsel on when to call 911 (slide 19).',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 14, 18–22'},

{id:'sp02', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-lice-age-product',
 tags:['star','pediculosis','age','retreatment'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A mother asks for a head lice product for her 18-month-old son. Nits and nit castings are seen at the crown and behind the ears. He has no sores from scratching, no lice in the eyebrows or eyelids, and no known allergies. Which recommendation fits?',
 options:[
  {t:'Synergized pyrethrins shampoo with a lice comb, and a required second treatment in 7–10 days', correct:false, why:'Slide 31 excludes pyrethrins for children under 2 years, and this child is 18 months old. The 7–10 day retreatment is correct for pyrethrins (slide 32), which is why this option looks right, but choosing it applies the pyrethrin age limit to the wrong product.'},
  {t:'Permethrin cream rinse for 10 minutes with a lice comb, and a second treatment only if lice or nits are seen at 7–10 days', correct:true, why:'Slide 31 excludes permethrins only under 2 months, so an 18-month-old may use permethrin (Nix). Slide 33: cream rinse for 10 minutes, use a lice comb, and retreat in 7–10 days only if lice or nits are seen. No other exclusion is present, so self-care is appropriate.'},
  {t:'Refer him to primary care, because every head lice product is excluded for children under 2 years of age, so none can be used', correct:false, why:'Slide 31 gives two different age cutoffs: under 2 years for pyrethrins and under 2 months for permethrins. Referring because of age under 2 years treats the pyrethrin cutoff as covering all products, a confusion between neighboring limits.'},
  {t:'Permethrin cream rinse for 10 minutes with a lice comb, and a required second treatment in 7–10 days', correct:false, why:'Permethrin is the right product for this age, but slide 33 says it may be retreated in 7–10 days if lice or nits are seen, while slide 32 says pyrethrins must be retreated. Choosing this option reverses the two retreatment rules.'}],
 teach:'Match the product to the age limit first, then the retreatment rule. Pyrethrins (synergized with piperonyl butoxide): excluded under 2 years; must be retreated in 7–10 days; comb required. Permethrin (Nix): excluded under 2 months; cream rinse for 10 minutes; comb required; retreat in 7–10 days only if lice or nits are seen. Other exclusions that send a patient to a provider: secondary infection from scratching, lice in the eyelids or eyebrows, hypersensitivity to chrysanthemums, ragweed or pediculicide ingredients, pregnancy or breastfeeding, active tumors and regional resistance (slide 31).',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 31–33'},

{id:'sp03', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-lice-breastfeeding',
 tags:['star','pediculosis','exclusions','breastfeeding'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A 29-year-old woman who is breastfeeding her 3-month-old daughter has had an itchy scalp for a week. Nits and live lice are seen at the crown. She has no scratch sores, no eyebrow or eyelid involvement and no allergies, and she asks for permethrin because a friend called it the mildest product. What is the best recommendation?',
 options:[
  {t:'Permethrin cream rinse for 10 minutes, retreating in 7–10 days only if lice or nits are seen', correct:false, why:'Permethrin is a reasonable product for most adults, but slide 31 lists pregnancy or breastfeeding as a strong caution for self-treatment with referral to primary care. Choosing it because she has no allergy and no age problem ignores the exclusion that applies to her.'},
  {t:'Synergized pyrethrins shampoo with a lice comb, retreating in 7–10 days', correct:false, why:'Slide 31 applies the breastfeeding caution to self-treatment in general, not to one product, so switching from permethrin to pyrethrins does not remove it. Choosing this reveals the belief that only some pediculicides are affected by pregnancy or breastfeeding.'},
  {t:'Do not choose a product; refer her to primary care for treatment', correct:true, why:'Slide 31 lists pregnancy or breastfeeding as a strong caution for self-treatment and says to refer to primary care. Her lack of allergies, scratch sores or eyelid lice does not matter, because one exclusion is enough to move her from self-care to a provider.'},
  {t:'Either product, because she has no allergy to chrysanthemums, ragweed or pediculicide ingredients', correct:false, why:'Slide 31 lists the hypersensitivity exclusion separately from the pregnancy or breastfeeding exclusion. A negative allergy history clears only the first, so this option checks one exclusion and misses the one that decides her case.'}],
 teach:'Work through every exclusion on slide 31 before choosing a product, because any single one sends the patient to a provider: secondary infection from scratching, hypersensitivity to chrysanthemums, ragweed or pediculicide ingredients, age under 2 years (pyrethrins) or under 2 months (permethrins), lice in the eyelids or eyebrows, pregnancy or breastfeeding, active tumors, and regional resistance to pediculicides. The SCHOLAR-MAC condition question (“are you currently pregnant or breastfeeding?”, slide 6) is where this exclusion is found, so ask it before recommending any product.',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 6, 31, 34'},

{id:'sp04', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-hydrocortisone-who',
 tags:['star','hydrocortisone','exclusions'], source:'slide',
 note:'Slide 27 says hydrocortisone 1% is applied 1-4 times a day and the APhA excerpt says 3 or 4 times daily; both say up to 7 days, so no option keys the frequency.',
 stem:'Which patients may use hydrocortisone 1% for insect bites without medical supervision? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'A 3-year-old with a minor insect bite on the leg', correct:true, why:'The APhA excerpt says external analgesics, hydrocortisone included, are not approved under 2 years, so a 3-year-old is within the approved age. Slide 27 indicates hydrocortisone 1% for temporary relief of minor insect bites, so no exclusion applies. Excluding him confuses the under-2 limit with a higher age.'},
  {t:'A 15-month-old with a minor insect bite', correct:false, why:'The APhA excerpt says external analgesics such as hydrocortisone are not approved for children younger than 2 years, and 15 months is under that age. Choosing it because the bite is minor treats slide 27’s indication as enough and misses the age restriction.'},
  {t:'A 40-year-old on day 5 of use for a minor bite, planning to stop by day 7', correct:true, why:'Slide 27 allows hydrocortisone 1% for up to 7 days and says to seek medical attention if it is needed longer. Day 5 with a plan to stop by day 7 stays inside that limit, so no supervision is needed. Excluding this patient mistakes any ongoing use for use beyond 7 days.'},
  {t:'A 52-year-old with itchy bites and diagnosed scabies', correct:false, why:'Slide 14 refers scabies to a medical provider, and the APhA excerpt says patients with scabies should not use hydrocortisone without medical supervision because it can worsen or mask the disorder. Choosing it treats itching as the only target, not the cause of the itch.'},
  {t:'A 35-year-old with a bite next to a fungal infection on the same foot', correct:false, why:'The APhA excerpt says patients with bacterial or fungal infections should not use hydrocortisone without medical supervision, because a steroid can worsen or mask the infection. Choosing it treats the bite and the infection as unrelated, when the steroid would be applied near the infected skin.'},
  {t:'A 28-year-old planning 10 days of daily use for a stubborn bite', correct:false, why:'Slide 27 says to apply hydrocortisone 1% for up to 7 days and to seek medical attention if it is needed longer, and the APhA excerpt says topical antipruritics should not be used more than 7 days unless a provider advises it. Ten days exceeds the self-care limit.'}],
 teach:'Hydrocortisone 1% is a very weak steroid for temporary relief of minor insect bites, for up to 7 days (slide 27). Three checks before recommending it: age (external analgesics are not approved under 2 years), duration (7 days, then medical attention) and the skin (no scabies, bacterial infection or fungal infection, because it can worsen or mask them; scabies is also a referral condition on slide 14). The number of applications per day is not keyed, because slide 27 and the APhA excerpt give different frequencies.',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 14, 27; APhA-Insect-Excerpt.pdf'},

{id:'sp05', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-soap-objective-case',
 tags:['star','SOAP'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist writes a SOAP note for a 7-year-old with head lice. Which entry belongs in the Objective section?',
 options:[
  {t:'The pharmacist sees black powdery specks at the base of the neck when the hair is parted', correct:true, why:'Slides 8–9: objective information is what you and others observing the patient would consider facts, including physical exam and visual inspection findings. Slide 30 says black powdery specks (lice feces) can be seen when the hair is parted, so the pharmacist’s own inspection belongs in Objective.'},
  {t:'The mother says she saw lice crawling at the crown last night', correct:false, why:'Slide 9 puts what the patient says, here the mother reporting for the child, under Subjective with SCHOLAR-MAC. The content describes lice at the crown, but the pharmacist did not observe it, so choosing it confuses what a finding is about with who observed it.'},
  {t:'Head lice infestation, with no exclusions to self-care', correct:false, why:'Slide 10 says the Assessment is your clinical interpretation of the condition with your rationale. A conclusion that this is head lice and that no exclusion applies is interpretation, not an observed fact, so choosing it mixes up a finding with what the finding means.'},
  {t:'Permethrin cream rinse for 10 minutes with a lice comb, retreating if lice or nits are seen in 7–10 days', correct:false, why:'Slide 9 puts what you will do, with follow-up instructions and education, in the Plan. A product and a retreatment interval are actions you recommend, not observations, so choosing this entry places a recommendation in the section meant for facts.'}],
 teach:'Sort by who observed it and whether it is a fact or a decision. Subjective: what the patient (or caregiver) says, collected with SCHOLAR-MAC. Objective: facts the pharmacist and others observing the patient at the same time would agree on, such as lab results, exam findings, visual inspection and procedure findings. Assessment: your interpretation and rationale. Plan: product, counseling, follow-up interval and location, and when to seek medical treatment (slides 8–10). In SOAP, what the caregiver reports is Subjective and what the pharmacist observes is Objective (slides 8–10); that is the reasoning applied here.',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 8–10, 30'},

{id:'sp06', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'otc', concept:'star-child-products-avoid',
 tags:['star','product selection','children'], source:'both',
 note:'Slide 26 says to keep camphor away from children and pets; the APhA excerpt says camphor products can be very dangerous if ingested and are an inappropriate choice for children. The camphor key follows the APhA excerpt.',
 stem:'An 8-year-old has pain and itching at a honeybee sting site, with no exclusion to self-care. Which products should be avoided for this child? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'A camphor-containing counterirritant', correct:true, why:'The APhA excerpt says camphor-containing products can be very dangerous if ingested, making them an inappropriate choice for children, and slide 26 says to keep camphor away from children and pets. Choosing a counterirritant because it relieves itching ignores that the risk is ingestion by a child.'},
  {t:'A pramoxine product', correct:false, why:'Slide 24 and the APhA excerpt say pramoxine and benzyl alcohol do not commonly cause adverse effects and show less cross-sensitivity than other local anesthetics. Avoiding it confuses the safest local anesthetic with the ones that carry warnings.'},
  {t:'A phenol-containing product', correct:true, why:'Slide 24 says not to use phenol in pregnancy or in infants or children, and the APhA excerpt says to avoid phenol in pregnant patients and children. A low phenol concentration does not remove this restriction, so the age of the patient decides it.'},
  {t:'Calamine lotion', correct:false, why:'Slide 28 says zinc oxide and calamine are recommended for adults, children and infants and may be applied as needed. Avoiding it confuses a skin protectant with the counterirritant and local anesthetic products that carry child warnings.'},
  {t:'Dibucaine', correct:true, why:'Slide 24 says to avoid dibucaine because of the risk of systemic toxicity, including seizures, respiratory depression, arrhythmias and death, and the APhA excerpt calls it a common allergen. Skin application seems safe, but the warning concerns absorption, so it is avoided in any patient.'},
  {t:'Hydrocortisone 1% for up to 7 days', correct:false, why:'Slide 27 indicates hydrocortisone 1% for temporary relief of minor insect bites for up to 7 days, and the APhA excerpt’s age limit is under 2 years, so an 8-year-old is within it. Avoiding it confuses a steroid with products that carry specific child warnings.'}],
 teach:'For a child with an uncomplicated sting, three groups of products carry warnings: camphor (very dangerous if ingested; inappropriate for children), phenol (not for pregnancy, infants or children; above 2% irritating and may cause necrosis; never under bandages or over large areas) and dibucaine (systemic toxicity). Better tolerated choices are pramoxine or benzyl alcohol, skin protectants (zinc oxide, calamine, as needed) and, for minor bites without scabies or infection, hydrocortisone 1% for up to 7 days. Topical antipruritics and anesthetics are not used more than 7 days without a provider.',
 cite:'OTC-Insect-Bites-Pediculosis.pdf slides 24, 26–28; APhA-Insect-Excerpt.pdf'},

/* ---- physical assessment and POCT ------------------------------------- */
{id:'sp07', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'poct', concept:'star-bp-errors',
 tags:['star','blood pressure','technique'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A student measures the blood pressure of a 52-year-old man who drank coffee 15 minutes ago. The cuff is snug over a thin sleeve, 1 inch above the elbow, with the artery marker over the brachial artery. The patient holds his own arm up at heart level. The student deflates at 2–3 mmHg per second, records the first tapping sound as systolic and the last sound as diastolic, then repeats the measurement after 2 minutes. Which steps are errors? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Taking the reading 15 minutes after coffee', correct:true, why:'Slide 35 says the patient should avoid caffeine, smoking and exercise for at least 30 minutes before the measurement, so 15 minutes is too short. The coffee detail is easy to overlook because the cuff, arm position, deflation rate and sound recording in the case look routine.'},
  {t:'Wrapping the cuff over a thin sleeve', correct:false, why:'Slide 34 says to place the cuff around the bicep and that over a shirt is ok. Choosing it as an error reveals a belief that the cuff must touch bare skin, which applies to the stethoscope diaphragm (slide 36), not to the cuff.'},
  {t:'Letting the patient hold his own arm up at heart level', correct:true, why:'Slide 34 says the arm should be at heart level but the patient should not attempt to support it, because muscle tension can falsely elevate the reading. Heart level is correct, so the error is who holds the arm; resting it on a support avoids that tension.'},
  {t:'Deflating the cuff at 2–3 mmHg per second', correct:false, why:'Slide 36 says to release air gradually at about 2–3 mmHg per second while listening for the first tapping sound. This is the correct rate, so choosing it as an error confuses it with a faster, less accurate release.'},
  {t:'Repeating the measurement after 2 minutes', correct:true, why:'Slide 37 says that if a repeat is warranted, wait 5 minutes. A repeat after only 2 minutes is 3 minutes short of that interval, so the second reading does not follow the technique.'},
  {t:'Recording the last sound as the diastolic pressure', correct:false, why:'Slide 37 says to listen until the tapping sounds disappear and that the last sound heard is the diastolic pressure, while the first clear tapping sound is the systolic (slide 36). Choosing it as an error swaps the two end points.'}],
 teach:'Check a blood pressure case in the order of the technique. Patient: seated, feet flat, arm at heart level and not self-supported, no caffeine, smoking or exercise for at least 30 minutes (slides 34–35). Cuff: correct size, snug, 1 inch above the elbow, artery marker over the brachial artery, over a shirt is ok; stethoscope diaphragm on bare skin below the cuff edge (slides 33–36). Measurement: inflate to about 160–180 mmHg, deflate 2–3 mmHg per second, first tapping sound systolic, last sound diastolic. Repeat only after waiting 5 minutes (slide 37).',
 cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 33–37'},

{id:'sp08', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'poct', concept:'star-pulse-resp-math',
 tags:['star','pulse','respirations','calculation'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist counts a radial pulse for 15 seconds and feels 25 beats. Then, while still holding the wrist, the pharmacist counts the patient’s breaths for 30 seconds and counts 11. Which interpretation and next step are correct?',
 options:[
  {t:'Pulse 100 beats/min is tachycardia; respiratory rate 22 breaths/min is normal', correct:false, why:'25 × 4 = 100 beats/min, and tachycardia is above 100 (slide 30), so 100 is not tachycardia. A respiratory rate of 22 is above 20, so it is tachypnea (slide 32), not normal. This option misreads both cutoffs by one step.'},
  {t:'Pulse 100 beats/min is within the normal range, so record it without a recount; respiratory rate 11 breaths/min is bradypnea', correct:false, why:'25 × 4 = 100 is normal on its face, but slide 31 says a number close to a cutoff should be recounted for a full minute. The 11 breaths are a 30-second count, so the rate is 11 × 2 = 22 breaths/min; forgetting to multiply produces the false bradypnea.'},
  {t:'Pulse 100 beats/min: recount for a full minute; respiratory rate 44 breaths/min, tachypnea', correct:false, why:'The pulse step is right: 25 × 4 = 100 is at the tachycardia cutoff, so slide 31 says to recount for a full minute. A 30-second breath count is multiplied by 2, not 4, so 11 breaths is 22 per minute, not 44.'},
  {t:'Pulse 100 beats/min is at the cutoff, so recount for a full minute; respiratory rate is 22 breaths/min, tachypnea', correct:true, why:'25 beats in 15 seconds × 4 = 100 beats/min, which is at the tachycardia cutoff (above 100), so slide 31 says to recount for a full minute. 11 breaths in 30 seconds × 2 = 22 breaths/min, above 20, which is tachypnea (slide 32).'}],
 teach:'Convert first, then compare with the cutoff. Pulse: count for 15 seconds and multiply by 4, or 30 seconds and multiply by 2; normal 60–100, tachycardia above 100, bradycardia below 60; if the result is close to a cutoff, recount for the full minute (slides 30–31). Respirations: watch the chest rise and fall without telling the patient, count for at least 30 seconds (so multiply by 2); normal 12–20, tachypnea above 20, bradypnea below 12 (slide 32). Write the multiplication before reading off the category.',
 cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 30–32; TOSCE-Study-Guide.pdf'},

{id:'sp09', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'poct', concept:'star-throat-swab',
 tags:['star','throat swab','technique'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist collects a throat swab for a rapid Group A strep test after telling the patient what to expect and having the patient tilt the head back, stick out the tongue and close the eyes. Which swab motion is correct?',
 options:[
  {t:'Begin at the uvula, strike each tonsil with the side of the swab, then swab back immediately', correct:false, why:'Slide 46 says to start at the base of the arch at the level of the tonsil and follow the glossopalatine arch, striking the uvula along the way. Starting at the uvula reverses the order of the landmarks, so the swab never covers the arch from one tonsil to the other.'},
  {t:'Begin at one tonsil, strike the uvula with the tip of the swab, end at the opposite tonsil, then remove the swab', correct:false, why:'Slide 46 says to strike the uvula with the side of the swab, not the tip, and to swab immediately in the reverse direction. This option has two errors, the tip and the missing return pass, so it collects a smaller specimen than the technique requires.'},
  {t:'Begin at the base of the arch at one tonsil, strike the uvula with the side of the swab, end at the opposite tonsil, then swab back immediately', correct:true, why:'Slide 46: start at the base of the arch at the level of the tonsil, follow the glossopalatine arch, strike the uvula with the side of the swab, end at the opposite tonsil, then immediately swab in the reverse direction. Each landmark and the return pass are required.'},
  {t:'Begin at the base of the arch at one tonsil, strike the uvula with the pointed tip of the swab, end at the opposite tonsil, then swab back immediately', correct:false, why:'Slide 46 says to strike the uvula with the side of the swab. Everything else in this option matches the technique, the tonsil start, the opposite tonsil and the immediate return pass, so choosing it reveals a mistake in only one detail, the tip in place of the side.'}],
 teach:'Throat swab sequence (slide 46): explain what to expect (coughing or gagging may occur), sanitize hands and put on appropriate PPE, head tilted back, tongue out, eyes closed, hold a tongue depressor in the non-dominant hand and the correct swab for the test in the dominant hand. Swab from the base of the arch at the level of the tonsil, follow the glossopalatine arch, strike the uvula with the side of the swab, end at the opposite tonsil, then immediately swab in the reverse direction. Nasal swab for comparison (slides 48–49): the patient does not blow the nose first; rotate the swab 5 times and leave it in the nostril for 15 seconds.',
 cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 46–49'},

{id:'sp10', skill:'error', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'poct', concept:'star-oximetry-reliability',
 tags:['star','pulse oximetry','skin tone'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist checks the oxygen saturation of a 52-year-old Black patient who reports shortness of breath. The patient wears acrylic nails, and the hands feel cold. The oximeter is placed on the index finger, and a reading of 96% is recorded after the patient has been still for 20 seconds, although the numbers were still changing. Which findings make the 96% unreliable? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Acrylic nails left on the finger', correct:true, why:'Slide 42 says to make sure the fingernails are dry and clean and to remove nail polish, acrylic nails or artificial tips before measuring. Leaving acrylic nails on is therefore a technique error, and the step is easy to miss because the finger site itself is correct.'},
  {t:'Placing the oximeter on the index finger', correct:false, why:'Slide 42 says to place the oximeter on the fingertip of either the index or the middle finger. Calling the index finger an error confuses an approved site with the other steps in the case that were done wrong.'},
  {t:'Cold hands', correct:true, why:'Slide 42 says to warm the hands if they are cold or cool to the touch before taking a reading. Skipping this step is a technique error even though the device displays a number, so the 96% cannot be trusted as recorded.'},
  {t:'Recording the reading after 20 seconds while the numbers were changing', correct:true, why:'Slide 42 says the patient should be still for at least a minute, longer if the numbers keep fluctuating, and the reading counts only once the numbers remain steady. A changing display at 20 seconds is not a final value.'},
  {t:'Darker skin pigmentation', correct:true, why:'Slide 41 says pulse oximeters overestimate oxygen saturation in Black patients because melanin absorbs more light, so readings can look normal when blood oxygen is dangerously low. Add this to the technique errors, because a normal 96% in a symptomatic patient is not reassuring.'},
  {t:'Recording both the saturation and the heart rate', correct:false, why:'Slide 42 says to record the two numbers shown on the device, the oxygen saturation and the heart rate, each clearly labeled. Recording both is the correct technique, so choosing it confuses a required step with an error.'}],
 teach:'A pulse oximetry result is trusted only after correct technique: dry, clean nails with polish and artificial nails removed, warm hands, the index or middle finger, and a still patient for at least a minute until the numbers are steady; record the saturation and the heart rate (slide 42). Normal is 95% to 99%, and below 90% needs medical attention (slide 40). In Black patients the oximeter overestimates saturation, so use a lower threshold for sending the patient to the ER, where an arterial blood gas can be obtained (slide 41).',
 cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 40–42'},

{id:'sp11', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'poct', concept:'star-strep-specificity',
 tags:['star','sensitivity','specificity','Group A strep'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacy starts rapid Group A strep testing for adults with a sore throat. Leadership worries that many patients without strep will receive antibiotics they do not need. Which test characteristic should the pharmacist look for, and what does it describe?',
 options:[
  {t:'High sensitivity: most people with strep test positive', correct:false, why:'Slide 55 defines sensitivity as the proportion of people with the disease who test positive. It protects against missed cases, not against treating people who do not have strep, so it answers the false-negative concern of slide 58, not leadership’s concern.'},
  {t:'High specificity: most people with strep test positive', correct:false, why:'Slide 55 defines specificity as the proportion of people without the disease who test negative. This option names the right property but attaches the definition of sensitivity to it, so it shows the two terms are mixed up even though the word is correct.'},
  {t:'High sensitivity: most people without strep test negative', correct:false, why:'This is the definition of specificity (slide 55) attached to the word sensitivity. Choosing it shows the two terms are swapped, which leads to selecting a test for the wrong error, missed cases rather than unnecessary treatment.'},
  {t:'High specificity: most people without strep test negative', correct:true, why:'Slide 55 defines specificity as the proportion of people without the disease who test negative, and slide 58 says a test with poor specificity could lead to unnecessary courses of treatment. Slide 19 notes that over 60% of adults with pharyngitis receive an antibiotic although about 10% are indicated.'}],
 teach:'Match the test property to the error you most want to avoid. Sensitivity: the proportion of people with the disease who test positive; high sensitivity means few missed cases (false negatives), which is why screening for HCV or HIV, where most people tested do not have the disease, needs high sensitivity (slide 57). Specificity: the proportion of people without the disease who test negative; high specificity means few false positives, so fewer people are treated unnecessarily (slide 58). Quick method: sensitivity protects the sick, specificity protects the healthy.',
 cite:'POCT-Physical-Assessment.pdf slides 19, 55–58'},

/* ---- immunizations ---------------------------------------------------- */
{id:'sp12', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-pneumo-after-ppsv23',
 tags:['star','pneumococcal','PPSV23'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A 44-year-old man with type 2 diabetes received 1 dose of PPSV23 eight months ago and has no other pneumococcal vaccine history. He asks whether he needs anything more. What do the schedule Notes recommend?',
 options:[
  {t:'Give 1 dose of PCV20 or PCV21 now, since PPSV23 alone does not complete the series', correct:false, why:'The Notes for previously receiving only PPSV23 give 1 dose of PCV15, PCV20 or PCV21 at least 1 year after the last PPSV23 dose. Eight months is four months short, so this option has the right product and the wrong timing.'},
  {t:'Wait until 1 year after the PPSV23 dose, then give 1 dose of PCV20 or PCV21', correct:true, why:'Diabetes mellitus is an underlying condition for ages 19–49, and the Notes for previously receiving only PPSV23 give 1 dose of PCV15, PCV20 or PCV21 at least 1 year after the last PPSV23 dose. PCV20 or PCV21 completes the pneumococcal vaccines, with no additional dose recommended.'},
  {t:'Give 1 dose of PCV15 now, then a second PPSV23 dose 8 weeks later', correct:false, why:'The 8-week minimum interval applies only to adults with an immunocompromising condition, a cochlear implant or a cerebrospinal fluid leak, and diabetes is not on that list. Also, after PCV15 following PPSV23 no additional PPSV23 dose is recommended, so both parts of this option fail.'},
  {t:'Nothing more is recommended, since diabetes alone is not an indication at age 44', correct:false, why:'The Notes list diabetes mellitus among the underlying medical conditions or other risk factors that qualify adults 19–49 for pneumococcal conjugate vaccine. Choosing this treats an older-adult age rule as the only route in and misses the condition-based route.'}],
 teach:'Pneumococcal vaccine decisions need two facts: the indication (age 50 or older, or age 19–49 with an underlying condition such as diabetes, chronic heart, liver or lung disease, alcoholism or smoking) and the exact history. After only PPSV23, give 1 dose of PCV15, PCV20 or PCV21 at least 1 year after that dose; PCV20 or PCV21 ends the series, and if PCV15 is used no additional PPSV23 is recommended. The shorter 8-week interval is only for immunocompromising conditions, a cochlear implant or a CSF leak. Always compute the earliest date before saying “today”.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes (Pneumococcal vaccination); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 8'},

{id:'sp13', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-hepb-dialysis-engerix',
 tags:['star','hepatitis B','dialysis'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A 66-year-old man with end-stage renal disease starts maintenance hemodialysis. He has never had hepatitis B vaccine, and the pharmacy stocks Engerix-B. Which plan follows the schedule Notes?',
 options:[
  {t:'3-dose Engerix-B series at 0, 1 and 6 months, using the standard adult 1 mL dose', correct:false, why:'The standard 3-dose Engerix-B series at 0, 1 and 6 months is the non-dialysis regimen. For patients on dialysis the Notes give a 3-dose Recombivax HB dialysis formulation or a 4-dose Engerix-B series with 2 mL per dose, so this is a correct schedule for the wrong patient.'},
  {t:'2-dose Heplisav-B series at 0 and 1 months', correct:false, why:'Heplisav-B is a 2-dose series for the general age 19–59 recommendation, but the Notes say patients on dialysis complete a 3- or 4-dose series. Choosing it ignores the dialysis row and applies the shortest series to the highest-risk patient.'},
  {t:'No hepatitis B vaccine, because the series is recommended only through age 59', correct:false, why:'The Notes say adults 60 or older with known risk factors for hepatitis B should receive a series, and maintenance dialysis is a listed risk factor. Table 1 shows yellow for 19 through 59 and light blue (additional risk factor) at 60 and older, and Table 2 shows dialysis as yellow, so this option misreads the schedule.'},
  {t:'4-dose Engerix-B series at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months, using 2 mL per dose', correct:true, why:'The Table 2 kidney failure and dialysis column is yellow for hepatitis B, and Table 1 shows light blue (additional risk factor) at 60 and older, so he should be vaccinated. For dialysis the Notes give a 4-dose Engerix-B series at 0, 1, 2 and 6 months using a 2 mL dose instead of the normal adult 1 mL.'}],
 teach:'Hepatitis B reasoning has three steps. First, is the patient in an age band or a risk group? Table 1 shows yellow for ages 19 through 59 and light blue (an additional risk factor) at 60 and older, Table 2 shows dialysis as yellow, and the Notes extend vaccination to adults 60 or older with risk factors (such as dialysis) and to any adult 60 or older who requests it. Second, is the patient on dialysis? Then use a 3- or 4-dose series: Recombivax HB dialysis formulation (1 mL = 40 mcg) at 0, 1, 6 months, or Engerix-B at 0, 1, 2, 6 months with 2 mL per dose. Third, match the brand to the stock on hand and read the dose volume.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes (Hepatitis B vaccination); Table 2'},

{id:'sp14', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-rzv-rsv-eligibility',
 tags:['star','zoster','RSV','age and risk'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which patients should receive Shingrix (RZV) or an RSV vaccine? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'A 58-year-old who had Zostavax in 2016 and shingles in 2021, asking about Shingrix', correct:true, why:'The Notes give a 2-dose RZV series for age 50 or older regardless of previous herpes zoster or previous zoster vaccine live (Zostavax). Prior shingles or Zostavax does not replace the recombinant series, so choosing against it confuses prior disease with prior protection.'},
  {t:'A 36-year-old with no chronic conditions, asking for Shingrix', correct:false, why:'RZV is routine from age 50 and, below that age, for immunocompromising conditions (Table 1; Notes). A healthy 36-year-old meets neither route, so choosing this applies the 19-and-older eligibility of immunocompromised adults to everyone.'},
  {t:'A 41-year-old with HIV and a CD4 count of 520 cells/mm³', correct:true, why:'The Notes recommend a 2-dose RZV series 2–6 months apart for immunocompromising conditions, including persons with HIV regardless of CD4 count. A high CD4 count matters for live vaccines such as MMR and varicella, not for this inactivated vaccine.'},
  {t:'A 68-year-old with no chronic conditions or other risk factors, asking for RSV vaccine', correct:false, why:'The Notes give RSV vaccine to adults 75 or older, and to ages 50–74 only with an increased risk of severe RSV disease. A healthy 68-year-old is in the 50–74 group without a listed risk, so choosing this applies the old-age rule too early.'},
  {t:'A 64-year-old with type 2 diabetes treated with insulin, never given RSV vaccine', correct:true, why:'For ages 50–74 the Notes recommend 1 dose of Arexvy, Abrysvo or mResvia when the patient is at increased risk, and diabetes requiring treatment with insulin or an SGLT2 inhibitor is listed. Patients can self-attest to a risk factor, so no further testing is needed. Table 2 shows the diabetes column for RSV as teal (shared clinical decision-making), but the Notes list the qualifying risk and decide the question.'},
  {t:'A 30-year-old at 20 weeks of pregnancy, asking for RSV vaccine', correct:false, why:'In pregnancy the Notes give 1 dose of Abrysvo only at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days, September through January, and say RSV vaccine is not recommended for all other pregnant persons. At 20 weeks the window has not opened.'}],
 teach:'Use the age first and the risk second. RZV (Shingrix): 2 doses 2–6 months apart (minimum 4 weeks) for age 50 or older regardless of prior shingles or Zostavax, and for immunocompromising conditions, including HIV at any CD4 count; no recommendation in pregnancy, so consider delaying. RSV: 1 dose for age 75 or older; 1 dose for ages 50–74 with increased risk (for example insulin- or SGLT2-treated diabetes, chronic heart or lung disease, end-stage renal disease); in pregnancy only Abrysvo at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days from September through January. Additional RSV doses are not recommended.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Table 1; Notes (Zoster vaccination; Respiratory syncytial virus vaccination)'},

{id:'sp15', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-pregnancy-live-hepa',
 tags:['star','pregnancy','live vaccines','hepatitis A'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 30-year-old woman at 14 weeks of pregnancy comes in during November. She has no evidence of immunity to rubella or varicella, has not had an influenza vaccine this season, and leaves in 3 weeks for a country with high endemic hepatitis A. Which vaccines may she receive today? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'FluMist (LAIV3)', correct:false, why:'LAIV3 is a live attenuated vaccine, and the Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication to it. Choosing it because she needs influenza vaccine this season confuses the need with the product; an inactivated influenza vaccine meets the need.'},
  {t:'Hepatitis A vaccine', correct:true, why:'Slide 14 says pregnant patients should receive hepatitis A vaccine in a special situation and that the vaccine is NOT live. The Notes list travel to a country with high or intermediate endemic hepatitis A as a risk, so travel in 3 weeks makes the dose indicated now.'},
  {t:'MMR', correct:false, why:'The Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication to MMR, a live vaccine. The Notes say a pregnant patient with no evidence of rubella immunity gets 1 dose after pregnancy, before discharge from the facility, so the missing immunity is a reason to wait, not to vaccinate today.'},
  {t:'Inactivated influenza vaccine (IIV3)', correct:true, why:'The Notes give 1 dose of an age-appropriate influenza vaccine annually for adults 19 and older, and the Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication only for the live LAIV3, not for the inactivated vaccines. Inactivated vaccine is therefore given during pregnancy.'},
  {t:'Abrysvo (RSV)', correct:false, why:'The Notes give Abrysvo in pregnancy only at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days, September through January, and say RSV vaccine is not recommended for all other pregnant persons. November matches the season but 14 weeks does not match the gestational window.'},
  {t:'Varicella (VAR)', correct:false, why:'The Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication to varicella vaccine, and the Notes say that with no evidence of immunity the first dose is given after pregnancy, before discharge from the facility. Missing immunity is the reason to defer a live vaccine, not to give it.'}],
 teach:'Sort vaccines by type before checking the indication. Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, LAIV3) are contraindicated in pregnancy; give the missing MMR or varicella dose after pregnancy, before discharge from the facility (slide 14). Inactivated vaccines such as hepatitis A are not live and can be given in pregnancy when a special situation applies (for example travel to a country with high or intermediate endemic hepatitis A). Pregnancy-specific vaccines: Tdap once in each pregnancy, preferably in the early part of weeks 27–36, and Abrysvo only at 32 weeks 0 days through 36 weeks 6 days from September through January.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 14; AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes (Hepatitis A, MMR, Varicella, RSV) and Appendix'},

{id:'sp16', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-tetanus-clean-wound',
 tags:['star','Tdap','wound management'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A 47-year-old man completed his tetanus primary series as a child, and his last tetanus-containing dose was a Tdap booster 8 years ago. He cuts a finger on a clean kitchen knife, and the wound is clean and minor. He asks whether he needs a tetanus shot today. What is correct?',
 options:[
  {t:'No tetanus vaccine today; the wound is clean and minor and the last dose was under 10 years ago', correct:true, why:'For persons with 3 or more tetanus-toxoid doses, the Notes give a dose for clean and minor wounds only if more than 10 years have passed since the last dose. Eight years is under 10, and the wound is clean and minor, so no dose is needed; the next routine Td or Tdap is due at 10 years.'},
  {t:'Tdap or Td today, because more than 5 years have passed since the last dose', correct:false, why:'The 5-year rule in the Notes applies to all other wounds, meaning not clean and minor. Choosing it applies the dirty-wound interval to a clean wound, a confusion between the two neighboring cutoffs of 5 and 10 years.'},
  {t:'A 3-dose catch-up series: Tdap now, Td or Tdap in 4 weeks, then another dose of Td or Tdap 6–12 months later', correct:false, why:'The catch-up series is for unvaccinated adults or an incomplete primary series, and this patient completed his primary series. Choosing it mixes up the primary-series catch-up with wound management and booster timing.'},
  {t:'Tdap only, because Tdap is preferred over Td for every wound', correct:false, why:'The Notes say Tdap is preferred when a dose is indicated and the patient has not previously received Tdap, but no dose is indicated here, and he already had Tdap. Product preference applies after the decision to vaccinate, so choosing it skips the decision step.'}],
 teach:'Wound management has two steps: decide whether a dose is due, then choose the product. With 3 or more previous tetanus-toxoid doses, give Tdap or Td for a clean and minor wound if more than 10 years since the last dose, and for all other wounds if more than 5 years. Then choose the product: Tdap is preferred if Tdap was never given or the history is unknown, and in pregnancy use Tdap. Patients with an incomplete primary series need the catch-up series instead. Quick method: classify the wound, take the last-dose date, compare with 10 (clean, minor) or 5 (all other).',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes (Tetanus, diphtheria and pertussis vaccination)'},

{id:'sp17', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-contra-vs-precaution',
 tags:['star','contraindications','precautions','live vaccines','HIV'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A pharmacist screens patients before live vaccines. Which findings are contraindications, meaning the vaccine should not be given, rather than precautions? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Varicella vaccine for a patient who is 12 weeks pregnant', correct:true, why:'The Appendix lists pregnancy as a contraindication to varicella vaccine, and footnote 1 says when a contraindication is present the vaccine should NOT be administered. The Notes defer the dose to after pregnancy, before discharge from the facility.'},
  {t:'FluMist (LAIV3) for a 30-year-old with asthma', correct:false, why:'The Appendix lists asthma in persons aged 5 years or older as a precaution for LAIV3, not a contraindication. Footnote 2 says vaccination should generally be deferred but might be indicated if the benefit outweighs the risk. Choosing it treats every listed condition as an absolute bar.'},
  {t:'MMR for a patient with HIV and a CD4 count of 150 cells/mm³', correct:true, why:'The Notes say MMR is contraindicated for HIV with a CD4 percentage under 15% or a CD4 count under 200 cells/mm³, and the Appendix lists severe immunodeficiency as a contraindication. A count of 150 is below 200, so the vaccine is not given.'},
  {t:'MMR for a patient who received an antibody-containing blood product 6 months ago', correct:false, why:'The Appendix lists receipt of an antibody-containing blood product within 11 months as a precaution for MMR, not a contraindication. The interval depends on the product, so the pharmacist checks the timing and may still vaccinate. Choosing it confuses a timing precaution with a bar.'},
  {t:'FluMist (LAIV3) for a patient with a cochlear implant', correct:true, why:'The Appendix lists a cochlear implant among the contraindications to LAIV3, together with asplenia, immunocompromise, pregnancy and a CSF leak. This item looks like a minor history detail, so it is easy to treat as a precaution when it is a contraindication.'},
  {t:'Varicella vaccine for a patient who takes aspirin daily', correct:false, why:'The Appendix lists use of aspirin or aspirin-containing products as a precaution for varicella vaccine, along with antiviral drugs within 24 hours before vaccination. Choosing it as a contraindication confuses a drug-timing precaution with the absolute reasons: allergy, severe immunodeficiency and pregnancy.'}],
 teach:'In the Appendix, a contraindication means the vaccine should NOT be given; a precaution means vaccination should generally be deferred but might be indicated if the benefit of protection outweighs the risk of an adverse reaction (footnotes 1 and 2). For live vaccines, contraindications are severe allergy, severe immunodeficiency (MMR and varicella are also contraindicated in HIV with CD4 under 15% or under 200 cells/mm³; either one is enough) and pregnancy; LAIV3 adds asplenia, cochlear implant and CSF leak. Precautions include moderate or severe acute illness, recent antibody-containing blood products, aspirin (varicella) and asthma (LAIV3, age 5 or older).',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Appendix; Notes (MMR, Varicella); Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slide 8'},

{id:'sp18', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-dose-too-early',
 tags:['star','dose interval','valid dose'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Several adult doses were given at these intervals. For each product on its standard schedule, the minimum interval between dose 1 and dose 2 is 4 weeks. Which doses count as valid and do not need to be repeated? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Shingrix dose 2, given 24 days after dose 1', correct:true, why:'The Notes say 4 weeks = 28 days and that doses given 4 days or fewer before the minimum interval are valid. 28 − 24 = 4 days early, which is within the 4-day allowance, so the dose counts. Shingrix is usually given 2–6 months after dose 1, but the minimum is 4 weeks.'},
  {t:'Shingrix dose 2, given 23 days after dose 1', correct:false, why:'28 − 23 = 5 days early. The Notes say doses given 5 or more days before the minimum interval are not valid and are repeated. One day separates this dose from the valid dose of 24 days, so a calendar count is needed rather than a rounded estimate.'},
  {t:'Heplisav-B dose 2, given 27 days after dose 1', correct:true, why:'28 − 27 = 1 day early. The Notes say a dose given 4 days or fewer before the minimum interval is valid, so the second dose counts and the 2-dose Heplisav-B series is complete. Repeating it would waste a dose.'},
  {t:'Heplisav-B dose 2, given 22 days after dose 1', correct:false, why:'28 − 22 = 6 days early, so the dose is invalid under the Notes (5 or more days early) and must be repeated. The repeat dose is spaced from the invalid dose by the recommended minimum interval, here 4 weeks.'},
  {t:'Twinrix dose 2, given 3 weeks after dose 1', correct:false, why:'Three weeks is 21 days, and 28 − 21 = 7 days early, so the dose is invalid. The Notes give a minimum of 4 weeks from Twinrix dose 1 to dose 2; the accelerated 0, 7, 21–30 day Twinrix schedule is a different schedule and the stem asks about the standard one.'}],
 teach:'Convert weeks to days (4 weeks = 28 days), subtract the interval actually given, and apply the grace period: a dose given 4 days or fewer before the minimum age or interval is valid; a dose given 5 or more days early is not valid and is repeated, spaced after the invalid dose by the recommended minimum interval (Notes, Additional information). Do not restart a series and do not add doses because the interval between doses was long. Quick method: 28 − days given = days early; 4 or less, keep; 5 or more, repeat.',
 cite:'AAFP-Adult-Schedule-2026.pdf Notes (Additional information; Zoster, Hepatitis B)'},

{id:'sp19', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', multi:true, tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-sbar-background',
 tags:['star','SBAR'], source:'both',
 stem:'A pharmacy intern writes an SBAR for the preceptor about a patient who came in for an influenza shot. Which statements belong in the Background? Select all that apply.',
 options:[
  {t:'Mr. Diaz is at the pharmacy for an influenza shot and a review of his vaccine needs', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 puts the reason the patient is at the pharmacy in the Situation, and (T) the situation should be something simple, such as the patient coming to the pharmacy. Choosing it as Background confuses why the patient is here with who the patient is.'},
  {t:'He is 52 years old with chronic liver disease and no hepatitis A vaccine on record', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 puts the age, conditions and vaccine history in the Background, and (T) these are the characteristics that back up or are required for the assessment. Chronic liver disease is the characteristic that makes hepatitis A vaccine relevant, so it belongs here.'},
  {t:'The AAFP Notes list chronic liver disease as a risk factor for hepatitis A, so vaccine is indicated', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 puts the reasoning that links the schedule to the patient, such as AAFP recommends PCV21 for patients aged 19–64 with diabetes, in the Assessment. A conclusion drawn from the schedule is interpretation, not background data.'},
  {t:'His last tetanus-containing vaccine was 4 years ago', correct:true, why:'Slide 22 includes the vaccine history, such as the influenza, Tdap and childhood vaccines of Mrs. Jones, in the Background. Past doses are patient characteristics that the assessment draws on, even when this one leads to no new tetanus dose.'},
  {t:'I recommend a 2-dose hepatitis A series, with Havrix doses 6–12 months apart', correct:false, why:'Slide 22 places what you recommend, such as I recommend PCV21, Shingrix, influenza, RSV and an updated COVID vaccine, in the Recommendation. A product and dose spacing are the action requested, not background.'}],
 teach:'SBAR sorting rule: Situation is one simple sentence about why the patient is here or why you are writing. Background holds the patient characteristics and history that the assessment relies on: age, conditions, relevant medications and vaccine history. Assessment states what the schedule says about this patient and why (the conclusion drawn from the background). Recommendation states exactly what you want done. (T) If an SBAR is hard to write, work backward from the recommendation: which assessment led to it, and which background facts support that assessment. Be complete but concise.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 20–22; 9/21 transcript (T)'},

{id:'sp20', skill:'apply', prof:'Dr. Parsons', tier:'new', topic:'starp', sub:'imm', concept:'star-ata-tell',
 tags:['star','vaccine hesitancy','Ask-Tell-Ask'], source:'both',
 stem:'A 54-year-old with type 2 diabetes declines the pneumococcal vaccine, saying, “I have a great immune system.” The pharmacist has asked why not and has asked permission to share information, and the patient agreed. Which response is the Tell step?',
 options:[
  {t:'Tell him the schedule recommends the vaccine for his age and diabetes, and ask him to sign a consent form today', correct:false, why:'Slide 32 says the Tell step is information centered on the patient’s belief or question, then listening and observing. Citing the schedule and asking for a signature does not address his belief that his immune system is strong, and it skips the listening part of the step.'},
  {t:'Explain that diabetes and age lower immunity, and that pneumococcal bacteria can reach the blood and brain', correct:true, why:'Slide 32: Tell is information centered on the patient’s belief, and (T) the pharmacist connects diabetes and aging to lower immunity and describes the serious complications, such as bacteria from the lungs reaching the blood and brain. This answers “great immune system” directly.'},
  {t:'Ask him again why he does not want the vaccine, then wait for a different answer', correct:false, why:'Slide 32 says the first Ask has already asked why not and obtained permission, so a second Ask repeats a completed step. Choosing it confuses the opening Ask with the Tell and gives him no information to weigh.'},
  {t:'Agree that his immune system is strong and tell him he can return if he changes his mind', correct:false, why:'Slide 32 uses this closing statement only at the end, after the final Ask, if the patient still declines. Using it at the Tell step ends the conversation before any information is shared, so it respects autonomy but skips the method.'}],
 teach:'Ask-Tell-Ask (slide 32): Ask, “It sounds like you don’t want the ___ shot today. Can you help me understand why not?” and “Do you mind if I share some information?” Tell, respond with information centered on the patient’s belief or question from reliable sources, then listen and observe verbal and nonverbal reactions and address further questions (slide 33: benefits shown in trials, risks). Ask again, “What are your thoughts now about getting this vaccine?” If the patient still declines, respect autonomy and say he can contact you if he wants to learn more or changes his mind. (T) Connect age and other diseases to lower immunity, and name the most serious complications.',
 cite:'Immunizations-Pre-Lab.pdf slides 32–33; 9/21 transcript (T)'}
);
